WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Jean-Jacques Rousseau’s philosophy&nbsp;
of education is based on the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.600
that children should develop a strong,&nbsp;
virtuous character, the ability to reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.620
and a refined sense of sentiment. This is so that,&nbsp;
as adults, they are able to resist temptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.480
think for themselves, and learn to&nbsp;
appreciate the finer things in life.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:33.660
To explain his ideas, Rousseau wrote “Emile, or&nbsp;
On Education”. The book tells the story of a boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:39.060
who goes through childhood, boyhood,&nbsp;
preadolescence, adolescence and adulthood.&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.320
All five stages happen under the&nbsp;
guardianship of his parents and a tutor.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:51.780
Stage 1, early childhood, begins with Émile’s&nbsp;
birth. During this stage, it is essential for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.760
parents to ensure that Émile develops physically.
Only the mother should feed the boy, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.760
she naturally loves him more than anyone else.
To allow the boy to make experiences with all his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.700
senses, he wears loose clothing and is placed in&nbsp;
a large cradle where he can move freely. Rousseau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.160
who was influenced by John Locke, believed that&nbsp;
dressing children too warmly makes them weak.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.140
If Emile doesn’t want to eat, he’s supported. If&nbsp;
he wants to use his left hand, he can. Beating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.880
or shouting at the boy should be avoided.
When Emile “cries”, which Rousseau called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:32.400
our first language, his mother should try to&nbsp;
understand him and find the underlying cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.500
Since children are born essentially&nbsp;
good, it is the responsibility of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.280
parents to shield them from those who&nbsp;
could influence their innocent minds.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:49.860
During Stage 2, Boyhood, Emile is educated to&nbsp;
perfect his senses. This is important because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.680
the mind needs a wide range of trained senses&nbsp;
to perceive information correctly and develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.320
good judgment. Instead of recommending books, the&nbsp;
tutor lets Emile play games and conduct natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:07.800
experiments outside. The city or a school are&nbsp;
not the right places for a child at this age.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:14.220
Emile learns to tell right from wrong&nbsp;
by experiencing the consequences of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.440
actions. Instead of actively teaching,&nbsp;
the tutor is merely a bystander who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.460
there to protect the boy from those&nbsp;
who want to influence his thinking or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.620
teach him bad habits. Over time Emile&nbsp;
learns morality and virtue naturally.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:35.280
In Stage 3, Preadolescence, Emile&nbsp;
is ready to learn how to reason.&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.920
Rousseau thought that only self-directed&nbsp;
learning led to the discipline necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.880
to acquire real knowledge.
The tutor therefore trusts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.800
Emile's interests and if the boy wants&nbsp;
to learn history, history he learns.&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.160
Languages are acquired&nbsp;
naturally through conversations.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:01.260
Emile is also taught carpentry, a manual&nbsp;
labor that requires creativity and intellect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.960
which helps him to become a virtuous adult who&nbsp;
knows the value of hard work. Doing such work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.900
also keeps Emile out of trouble, and allows him to&nbsp;
make a living in the event of a change in fortune.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:20.700
In Stage IV, Adolescence, Emile is ready for&nbsp;
the last part of his education — sentiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.520
Only now is he capable of understanding&nbsp;
complex emotions such as true compassion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:34.500
Emile now becomes a loving person who learns to&nbsp;
live with, and for others. Or as Rousseau wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:41.100
"We have made an active and thinking being. It&nbsp;
remains for us, in order to complete the man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:48.180
only to make a loving and feeling being—that&nbsp;
is to say, to perfect reason by sentiment"

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.060
The tutor now begins formal education&nbsp;
and introduces the passionate boy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.560
concepts such as religion. Children&nbsp;
who learn such complex ideas earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.320
are unable to think about them freely and hence&nbsp;
just recite what they have been told. Emile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.840
whose character has grown strong enough to reason,&nbsp;
is now permitted to mingle with people in society.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:17.580
In Stage V, Adulthood, Emile becomes a&nbsp;
virtuous man, who can make up his own mind.&nbsp;

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.540
He visits the city and develops a&nbsp;
sense for the finer things in life.&nbsp;

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.460
Thanks to his strong character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.360
the pursuit of wealth or other&nbsp;
temptations are none of his interest.&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.700
The young man is now ready to meet Sophie&nbsp;
— the perfect woman, his wife-to-be.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.600
Sophie, a representative of the ideal&nbsp;
woman, is educated to be governed by her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.480
husband while Émile, a representative of the&nbsp;
ideal man, is educated to be self-governing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.580
“The man should be strong and active; the woman&nbsp;
should be weak and passive”’, Rousseau wrote.&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.840
Sophie’s purpose is to please,&nbsp;
advise and console her husband.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:03.300
Once published, Rousseau’s books were widely&nbsp;
criticized, burned, and banned. The Church and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.880
state issued a warrant for Rousseau's arrest.&nbsp;
In addition, modern women disagreed with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.720
ideas or saw in him a hypocrite: Rousseau himself&nbsp;
sent away all five of his children to orphanages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:22.560
Nevertheless, “Emile” became Rousseau’s most&nbsp;
important work and a pillar of modern education.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:30.240
But what are your thoughts? Do you think Emile&nbsp;
receives the ideal education to become a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.040
free thinking mind? Is it right to try to&nbsp;
protect children from society's influence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.020
Or was Rousseau wrong about education? Tell&nbsp;
us what you think in the comments below.

